-- A TYPICALLY default on a rule's input that is not a literal gets the same
-- error as one on a record's field: "must be a literal", with the hint that
-- points at an OTHERWISE branch and a MAYBE input (HAIRCUT, ruled 2026-10-12).
-- Three shapes, each naming something that is in this file: a definition, a
-- sibling input, and a call.
--
-- A rule's signature is read before the definitions after it are in scope, so
-- each of the three is ALSO told "I could not find a definition", which is not
-- true of a name that is in the file. That sentence was said before a default
-- could be left out, and is not changed here. What this file pins is that the
-- literal error and its hint are not lost behind it.
-- not-ok/tc/typically-unresolved.l4 pins a name that is in the file nowhere.

GIVEN base IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY g
GIVETH A NUMBER
`from a definition` MEANS base

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY a
GIVETH A NUMBER
`from a sibling input` MEANS a PLUS b

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY (h 3)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`from a call` MEANS n

GIVETH A NUMBER
g MEANS 3

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
h MEANS x TIMES 2
